// Practice quiz: Exceptions and logging
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: python/python-fundamentals/exceptions-and-logging

::o1 specific except::You call int(user_text) and want to handle only text that is not a number. Which handler fits? {
~except\: #Not quite. A bare except also hides unrelated bugs and even KeyboardInterrupt.
~except NumberFormatException\: #Not quite. That is Java's name; Python raises ValueError, and this name would not even exist.
~catch (ValueError e)\: #Not quite. catch is Java syntax; Python uses except.
=except ValueError\: #Correct. int() raises ValueError for text that is not a whole number, so catch exactly that.
}

::o1 else clause::In try / except / else / finally, when does the else block run? {
~Only when an exception was caught #Not quite. That is the except block.
~Always, like finally #Not quite. finally always runs; else does not.
=Only when the try block raised no exception #Correct. else holds the code that should run after a successful try.
~Only when an exception escaped uncaught #Not quite. In that case else is skipped.
}

::o1 finally with an escaping error::A try block raises KeyError, the only handler is except ValueError, and there is a finally block. What happens? {
=finally runs, then the KeyError travels on to the caller #Correct. No handler matches, so the error propagates, but finally runs first.
~except ValueError catches it #Not quite. A handler only catches its own type and its subclasses; KeyError is not a ValueError.
~The program silently continues after the try #Not quite. An uncaught exception is never silently ignored.
~finally is skipped #Not quite. finally runs whether or not an exception occurred.
}

::o2 raise with a message::Inside def set_age(age), how do you reject a negative age with a clear error? {
~throw new ValueError("age must be 0 or more") #Not quite. throw new is Java; Python uses raise.
~return ValueError("age must be 0 or more") #Not quite. Returning an exception object does not raise it.
~raise "age must be 0 or more" #Not quite. Only exception objects can be raised; a string gives TypeError.
=raise ValueError("age must be 0 or more") #Correct. raise an exception instance with a message describing the problem.
}

::o2 custom class::Which line defines a custom exception that callers can catch by name? {
~class PaymentDeclined\: pass #Not quite. A class that does not derive from BaseException cannot be raised.
~def PaymentDeclined(Exception)\: pass #Not quite. def defines a function, not a class.
=class PaymentDeclined(Exception)\: pass #Correct. User-defined exceptions derive from Exception.
~exception PaymentDeclined #Not quite. exception is not a Python keyword.
}

::o2 no checked exceptions::How do Python exceptions differ from Java's checked exceptions? {
~Python requires a throws clause on every def #Not quite. Python has no throws clause at all.
=Python has no throws clause, and callers are never forced to catch #Correct. Any exception can propagate; an uncaught one stops the program with a traceback.
~Python checks at import time that every exception is caught #Not quite. Nothing is checked before the code runs.
~Python exceptions cannot carry messages #Not quite. Exceptions take a message argument.
}

::o3 module logger::What is the usual way for a module to get its own logger? {
=logger \= logging.getLogger(__name__) #Correct. Naming the logger after the module shows where each message came from.
~logger \= print #Not quite. print writes plain output with no levels or configuration.
~logger \= logging.basicConfig() #Not quite. basicConfig configures logging once at the entry point and returns None.
~logger \= Logger() #Not quite. Loggers are obtained with logging.getLogger, not created directly.
}

::o3 levels::The program calls logging.basicConfig(level\=logging.WARNING). Which call's message appears? {
~logger.info("started") #Not quite. INFO is below WARNING, so it is dropped.
=logger.error("disk full") #Correct. ERROR is above the WARNING threshold, so it is shown.
~logger.debug("x \= 3") #Not quite. DEBUG is the lowest level and is dropped.
~None of them #Not quite. Messages at WARNING or above appear.
}

::o3 log the traceback::Inside an except block, which call logs the error at ERROR level together with its traceback? {
=logger.exception("could not load file") #Correct. exception() logs at ERROR and adds the traceback of the exception being handled.
~logger.info("could not load file") #Not quite. This uses INFO and adds no traceback.
~print("could not load file") #Not quite. print has no level and no traceback.
~logger.debug(traceback) #Not quite. traceback is not defined here, and DEBUG is usually filtered out.
}
